      <h2>What Childwall Family Homes Need</h2>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1.5rem;">
        <p>Childwall is one of south Liverpool's strongest family-home suburbs. A 3-bed semi sells for £250,000 to £350,000. A 4-bed detached on Burns Close or Sulgrave Close can reach £500,000 and up. The streets are wide. The gardens are big. The schools are good.</p>

        <p>The housing is mostly 1930s and 1950s semis. Solid brick, bay windows, three or four bedrooms. These homes were built better than the Victorian terraces of inner Liverpool. The pipework is from the 1930s onwards, much of it part-upgraded over the years.</p>

        <p>The big gardens are the catch. Mature trees mean roots in the clay drain runs. The drains are now 80 to 90 years old. Roots find the joints. A blockage cleared today can come back in months if the roots aren't dealt with. We run a CCTV survey to show where the trouble is.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="pf-card pf-card-left-orange" style="margin-top:1rem;">
        <p>Childwall has good landmarks for local proof. Childwall Abbey Hotel on Childwall Abbey Road. Childwall Golf Club. Childwall Woods and Fields. Liverpool Hope University's Hope Park campus is just south.</p>

        <p>The school catchment drives strong family demand. Childwall Sports and Science Academy, the Bluecoat School nearby in Wavertree. Family bathrooms get refreshed every 10 to 15 years. Shower mixers wear out. Combi boilers get swapped after 12 to 15 years. We do all of it.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
